import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.Ke88N3OB.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Pumpkin Market API","description":"Market listing lookup, metadata updates, current publisher behavior and API findings","frontmatter":{"navigation":{"category":"Server operations"},"title":"Pumpkin Market API","description":"Market listing lookup, metadata updates, current publisher behavior and API findings","editLink":"https://github.com/filiphsps/pumpkin-plugins/edit/master/docs/pumpkin-market-api.md"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guides/pumpkin-market-api.md","filePath":"docs/pumpkin-market-api.md","lastUpdated":1792252051000}`),a={name:`guides/pumpkin-market-api.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="pumpkin-market-api" tabindex="-1">Pumpkin Market API <a class="header-anchor" href="#pumpkin-market-api" aria-label="Permalink to “Pumpkin Market API”">​</a></h1><p>This page brings together the Market API behavior relevant to this repository&#39;s publisher, the Market frontend, and PPM. It also records calls tested against the unpublished <code>TemporaryAPIExperiment</code> listing on 2026-10-07. No API token is stored here.</p><h2 id="listing-lookup" tabindex="-1">Listing lookup <a class="header-anchor" href="#listing-lookup" aria-label="Permalink to “Listing lookup”">​</a></h2><p>The public REST API is rooted at <code>https://market.pumpkinmc.org/api/v1/rest</code>.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Request</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>GET /plugins/{id}</code></td><td>Resolve a numeric database ID or the listing&#39;s public ID to its listing metadata.</td></tr><tr><td><code>GET /plugins?q={name}&amp;limit={n}</code></td><td>Search listings by name. Confirm an exact name match; fail if it is ambiguous.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The response includes the numeric <code>id</code>, <code>public_id</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>category</code>, <code>version</code>, and other basic listing information. PPM can resolve a plugin by public ID, numeric ID, exact name, or author/name input. This repository&#39;s publisher searches a bounded set of results and accepts a name only when it finds a case-insensitive exact match. In one test, <code>GET /plugins/TemporaryAPIExperiment</code> returned 404 while search found the listing. That single result doesn&#39;t establish how direct lookup behaves for every name.</p><p>The publisher action accepts either a plugin name or a numeric/public ID. For a name, it searches first, then uses the listing&#39;s numeric database ID for the upload endpoint.</p><p>Both Market actions cap successful listing-lookup JSON responses at 1 MiB. If a response exceeds that limit, the action fails before uploading a plugin or changing listing metadata.</p><p>The public API documentation covers read endpoints such as plugin lookup and download. It doesn&#39;t document the developer listing-write endpoint described below.</p><h2 id="updating-listing-metadata" tabindex="-1">Updating listing metadata <a class="header-anchor" href="#updating-listing-metadata" aria-label="Permalink to “Updating listing metadata”">​</a></h2><p>When you save the Store Listing form in the Market frontend, it sends:</p><div class="language-http"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">http</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark" style="--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark:#e1e4e8;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;--shiki-dark-bg:#24292e;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#c62739;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">PUT</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> /api/plugins/{numeric-database-id}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#11782a;--shiki-dark:#85E89D;">Authorization</span><span style="--shiki-light:#c62739;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">:</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> Bearer &lt;Market API token&gt;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#11782a;--shiki-dark:#85E89D;">Content-Type</span><span style="--shiki-light:#c62739;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">:</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> multipart/form-data</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The multipart form includes a <code>metadata</code> part containing JSON with these fields:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Shape</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>name</code></td><td>Listing display name</td></tr><tr><td><code>category</code></td><td>Market category</td></tr><tr><td><code>sourceLink</code></td><td>Optional source URL</td></tr><tr><td><code>youtubeVideoUrl</code></td><td>Optional video URL</td></tr><tr><td><code>keywords</code></td><td>Search keywords</td></tr><tr><td><code>translatedDescriptions</code></td><td>Object mapping locale codes to description strings; the frontend&#39;s default locale is <code>en-US</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>isEarlyAccess</code></td><td>Boolean</td></tr><tr><td><code>commands</code></td><td>Ordered array of command entries described below</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Each command entry may contain <code>id</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>aliases</code> (an array), <code>permission</code>, <code>description</code> (a locale map), and <code>display_order</code>. When saving, the frontend includes the database <code>id</code> for existing command rows and sends the complete command array. You can also include <code>preview_image</code> as a separate multipart file field. Screenshot uploads and deletes use their own endpoints, outside this metadata update flow.</p><p>For a metadata-only update, you don&#39;t need a <code>wasm</code> part. When sending <code>fetch</code> with <code>FormData</code>, let the runtime set the multipart <code>Content-Type</code> and its boundary. The frontend authenticates with a cookie and sends <code>X-Requested-With</code>. This repository&#39;s publish action uses an API token in the Bearer header instead.</p><p>The <code>publish-to-pumpkin-market</code> action uses the same <code>PUT</code> URL, but includes a <code>wasm</code> file and a different <code>metadata</code> object with <code>version</code>, <code>track</code>, and <code>releaseNotes</code>. Its README lists the token scopes used for version uploads. The test below confirms that its token could update the test listing; it doesn&#39;t identify the minimum scope for metadata changes. The frontend also displays a 2FA requirement when saving a listing or publishing a version. We haven&#39;t determined whether API-token writes also require the account to have 2FA enabled.</p><h2 id="partial-and-full-listing-updates" tabindex="-1">Partial and full listing updates <a class="header-anchor" href="#partial-and-full-listing-updates" aria-label="Permalink to “Partial and full listing updates”">​</a></h2><p>In the frontend flow we observed, both partial edits and full listing saves use <code>PUT /api/plugins/{id}</code>; there is no separate <code>PATCH</code> request. The caller controls the scope of the update by choosing which keys to include in the JSON <code>metadata</code> part.</p><p>In the API test, a partial object with <code>translatedDescriptions</code> and <code>commands</code> left the listing&#39;s existing <code>name</code> and <code>category</code> unchanged when read through the public REST endpoint. The endpoint also accepted a full listing object containing <code>name</code>, <code>category</code>, <code>sourceLink</code>, <code>youtubeVideoUrl</code>, <code>keywords</code>, <code>translatedDescriptions</code>, <code>isEarlyAccess</code>, and <code>commands</code>. A later partial update left the category from that full update unchanged. These results show that the same metadata-only <code>PUT</code> can be used for both patch-style edits and complete listing updates.</p><p>We haven&#39;t fully verified how the backend reconciles command rows. The write endpoint accepted command arrays, but the public REST response doesn&#39;t include descriptions or command rows. A Bearer-authenticated <code>GET /api/plugins/{id}</code> for the unpublished draft returned <code>This plugin is currently offline / draft.</code> Although the frontend preserves command IDs, we haven&#39;t confirmed how the backend handles omitted IDs, omitted commands, or deletion.</p><h2 id="tested-behavior" tabindex="-1">Tested behavior <a class="header-anchor" href="#tested-behavior" aria-label="Permalink to “Tested behavior”">​</a></h2><p>Every write below targeted the unpublished <code>TemporaryAPIExperiment</code> listing. The test uploaded no WASM binary and made no changes to any other listing.</p><ul><li>Public detail lookup by numeric database ID and public ID both returned the same listing and numeric database ID.</li><li>Direct lookup by name returned 404; a bounded search returned the exact listing.</li><li>A Bearer-authenticated metadata-only <code>PUT</code> containing a localized description and one command returned HTTP 200 with <code>{&quot;id&quot;:…, &quot;status&quot;:&quot;updated&quot;, &quot;version&quot;:&quot;&quot;}</code>.</li><li>A full listing metadata <code>PUT</code> also returned HTTP 200. The public REST readback showed the updated category and retained the listing name.</li><li>A subsequent partial metadata <code>PUT</code> returned HTTP 200 and left the category unchanged.</li><li>The listing stayed offline/draft, and no binary version was published. After metadata-only writes, its public <code>version</code> changed from <code>null</code> to an empty string. The fixture has no published version, so this doesn&#39;t tell us whether metadata-only writes preserve version data on a listing that has already been published.</li><li>The test fixture is left unpublished with category <code>Other</code> and the API probe description/command metadata.</li></ul><p>The frontend code confirms the request shape and command fields. The API tests don&#39;t confirm whether commands persist, how command deletion works, or whether metadata-only writes preserve version and track data on a listing that has already been published.</p><h2 id="references" tabindex="-1">References <a class="header-anchor" href="#references" aria-label="Permalink to “References”">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="https://github.com/Pumpkin-MC/Market-Frontend/blob/master/src/api.ts" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Market frontend API client</a></li><li><a href="https://github.com/Pumpkin-MC/Market-Frontend/blob/master/src/pages/dashboard/plugin/StoreListing.tsx" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Market frontend Store Listing editor</a></li><li><a href="https://github.com/Pumpkin-MC/Market-Frontend/blob/master/src/types/plugin.ts" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Market frontend plugin types</a></li><li><a href="https://github.com/Pumpkin-MC/ppm/blob/main/src/market/client.rs" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">PPM market API client</a></li><li><a href="https://github.com/Pumpkin-MC/ppm/blob/main/src/market/models.rs" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">PPM market response models</a></li><li><a href="https://market.pumpkinmc.org/api/docs/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Pumpkin Market API docs</a></li><li><a href="/pumpkin-plugins/actions/publish-to-pumpkin-market/README">This repository&#39;s Market publisher</a></li><li><a href="/pumpkin-plugins/guides/ci-and-releases#publishing-to-marketpumpkinmcorg">CI and release workflow</a></li></ul>`,27)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};